title: Why duplicates are not compared byte by byte
tags: Duplicates
kind: rationale
audience: dev
summary: Within a size class an accidental xxHash3-64 collision is far less likely than a read error in the comparison meant to rule it out.

The content hash is xxHash3-64, not a cryptographic hash, which raises the
question of whether a group can hold two files that are not identical. For an
''accidental'' collision, no, by a wide margin.

Files are only compared within a size class, so the birthday bound is over the
members of one class, not the repository: for `k` files of equal size and
distinct content, the chance that any two collide is about `k² / 2·2⁶⁴` —
3 × 10⁻¹⁶ at `k = 100`, 3 × 10⁻¹² at `k = 10 000`; even a million distinct files
of one size stay at 3 × 10⁻⁸.

That is orders of magnitude below the risk of the ''check itself'': at a typical
unrecoverable read-error rate of 10⁻¹⁵ per bit, re-reading a terabyte to compare
two files carries about a 10⁻³ chance of a read error. A byte-for-byte
confirmation pass would be more dangerous than the risk it claims to remove, so
the scan does not do one.

What "not cryptographic" does mean is that a collision can be ''manufactured''
cheaply: someone could plant a file crafted to collide with one of yours, so that
a dedup action offers to remove the original. That is a documented limit, not a
defended one. The usual mitigation, a secret per-repository hash seed, is
excluded by design: sync compares `mfr_full_hash` across two repositories as a
content signature, so the hash must not depend on the repository. Moving to a
keyed or cryptographic hash (BLAKE3) would change a persisted format, hence need
a migration.
